<PageLayout
  title="Multiple sclerosis relapses: why an ACTH gel is still on the label"
  seoTitle="MS relapses and repository corticotropin"
  kicker="The condition"
  description="Repository corticotropin, a long-acting ACTH gel, is labelled for acute exacerbations of multiple sclerosis in adults. High-dose corticosteroids are the usual relapse treatment. A small placebo-controlled trial in people who had not responded to steroids found more disability-scale responders on the gel. ACTH is a melanocortin; how much of the effect is cortisol, and how much is direct, is still debated."
  seoDescription="Acthar, an ACTH gel, is labelled for acute MS relapses. A 35-person trial in steroid non-responders favoured it on one disability scale. The mechanism is still partly cortisol."
  thread="A relapse is a flare of inflammation in the central nervous system. The oldest melanocortin drug is still licensed to quiet one — mostly, everyone agrees, by making the adrenal release cortisol."
  updated="2026"
  about={[
    { name: 'Multiple sclerosis', type: 'MedicalCondition', sameAs: ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Multiple_sclerosis'] },
    { name: 'Adrenocorticotropic hormone', type: 'MedicalEntity', sameAs: ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Adrenocorticotropic_hormone'] },
  ]}
>
  <KeyTakeaways
    items={[
      'The US label for repository corticotropin (Acthar Gel) includes acute exacerbations of multiple sclerosis in adults. It is not a disease-modifying MS drug.',
      'ACTH is a POMC peptide. Its established action is to drive adrenal MC2R and release cortisol, so the gel is at least an indirect steroid.',
      'A direct anti-inflammatory action through melanocortin receptors on immune cells is proposed and not settled.',
      'In a 35-person placebo-controlled trial of relapses that had not responded to high-dose steroids, about 61% on the gel versus about 12% on placebo were disability-scale responders at day 42. A symptom scale did not separate.',
      'Usual care for a relapse that needs treatment is high-dose corticosteroid. This page is the biology and the evidence, not a protocol.',
    ]}
  />

  <p>
    Multiple sclerosis is an immune disease of the central nervous system. A{' '}
    <strong>relapse</strong> is a new or worsening neurological deficit that lasts, caused by
    a fresh focus of inflammation. It is not, by itself, a melanocortin disease. It is on
    this site because repository corticotropin — ACTH in a slow-release gel — is
    FDA-labelled for the treatment of acute exacerbations of MS in adults.<Cite n={1} /> The
    same preparation’s other famous inflammatory use is{' '}
    <a href="/sarcoidosis">symptomatic sarcoidosis</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>An indirect steroid, and maybe something else</h2>
  <p>
    ACTH is cut from <a href="/pomc">POMC</a>. Injected, it binds{' '}
    <a href="/receptors/mc2r">MC2R</a> on the adrenal cortex and the cortex releases
    cortisol. For most of the drug’s history that was the whole explanation: Acthar is a
    way of making the patient produce their own steroid.<Cite n={3} /> High-dose
    corticosteroids are still the standard drug for an MS relapse that needs treating. The
    gel sits beside that, not in front of it, and its price has been a long-running
    argument of its own.<Cite n={3} />
  </p>
  <p>
    The melanocortin-specific claim is the extra one. Receptors of this family are also
    expressed on immune cells, and a steroid-independent anti-inflammatory effect has been
    invoked to explain why an ACTH preparation might help when a steroid already has not.
    The <a href="/therapeutics#corticotropin">therapeutics page</a> keeps the score on that
    debate. It is plausible pharmacology. It is not, on current evidence, a proven second
    mechanism you can point to in a patient.
  </p>

  <h2>The trial in steroid non-responders</h2>
  <p>
    The study that matches the “when steroids fail” use (NCT03126760) enrolled adults with
    relapsing-remitting MS whose relapse had not responded adequately to high-dose
    corticosteroids. Thirty-five people were randomised: 18 to 80 units of the gel daily for
    14 days, 17 to placebo. At day 42, 61% of the gel group and 12% of the placebo group met
    the disability-scale (EDSS) responder definition. A clinician global impression leaned
    the same way. The MS Impact Scale did not show a meaningful difference, and there were
    no serious adverse events in the report.<Cite n={2} />
  </p>
  <p>
    Read the size. Thirty-five people, one positive scale, one scale that did not move.
    That is evidence the gel can do something in a relapse that shrugged off steroids. It is
    not evidence that it should replace corticosteroids, and it says nothing about
    preventing the next relapse. Disease-modifying MS drugs are a different category
    entirely, and none of them is a melanocortin.
  </p>

  <h2>The honest bottom line</h2>
  <p>
    Acute MS relapse is a labelled use of an old ACTH drug. The melanocortin content is the
    peptide and the receptor that turns it into cortisol, plus an unresolved claim about
    immune-cell receptors. For the hormone, see <a href="/acth">ACTH</a>; for the receptor,{' '}
    <a href="/receptors/mc2r">MC2R</a>; for every other condition on this map,{' '}
    <a href="/conditions">the conditions page</a>.
  </p>

  <div class="aside-note not-prose">
    <p class="font-medium text-[var(--text)]">Education, not a protocol</p>
    <p class="mt-2 text-[var(--text)]">
      This page explains a labelled use of repository corticotropin. It is not medical
      advice and gives no dosing or treatment instructions. An MS relapse needs clinical
      care. See the <a href="/about">editorial standards</a>.
    </p>
  </div>

  <FAQ items={faqs} />

  <References items={references} />

  <RelatedPages slug="multiple-sclerosis-relapse" />
</PageLayout>
